---
import LegalPage from "../../components/pages/LegalPage.astro";
import { t, localePath } from "../../scripts/i18n.ts";

const lang = 'en' as const;
const breadcrumbItems = [
  { name: t(lang, "breadcrumb.home"), item: localePath(lang, "") },
  { name: t(lang, "breadcrumb.privacy"), item: localePath(lang, "privacy") },
];
---

<LegalPage
  lang={lang}
  metaTitle={t(lang, "meta.title.privacy")}
  metaDescription={t(lang, "meta.description.privacy")}
  title="Privacy Policy"
  crumbs={breadcrumbItems}
  tocLabel="Contents"
  testid="legal-privacy"
>
    <p class="legal-note"><strong>Note:</strong> The German version of this policy is binding. This translation is for information only.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Effective date:</strong> October 2026<br>
      WhisPaste is a desktop application for Windows, macOS, and Linux that transcribes speech to text. This policy explains what data the application processes, where it is stored, and what, if anything, is transmitted.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>1. Privacy at a Glance</h2>
      <p>
        The following information provides a simple overview of what happens to your personal data when you visit this website. Personal data is any data that can be used to identify you personally.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>2. Data Controller</h2>
      <p>
        Silvio Lindstedt und Maik Gräfendorf, GbR<br>
        Pappelweg 27<br>
        39576 Stendal<br>
        Email: kontakt@whispaste.de
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>3. Hosting</h2>
      <p>
        This website is hosted on <strong>GitHub Pages</strong>, a service provided by GitHub Inc. (88 Colin P. Kelly Jr. St., San Francisco, CA 94107, USA), a subsidiary of Microsoft Corporation.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you visit this website, GitHub automatically collects the following data:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>IP address</li>
        <li>Browser type and version</li>
        <li>Referrer URL</li>
        <li>Time of access</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is technically necessary for operating the website. The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR (legitimate interest). GitHub processes this data in accordance with the <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Privacy Statement</a>. Data transfers to the USA are safeguarded by <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-data-protection-agreement" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Standard Contractual Clauses (SCC)</a> pursuant to Art. 46(2)(c) GDPR.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>GitHub stars in the navigation:</strong> Your browser loads the displayed GitHub star count from our own interface <code>api.whispaste.de</code> (netcup GmbH, Germany, see section&nbsp;17), not directly from GitHub. The interface itself asks GitHub for the number at most every 15&nbsp;minutes and passes on no data about you. For technical reasons the server receives your IP address to deliver the response; the interface does not store it. The legal basis is Art.&nbsp;6(1)(f) GDPR (legitimate interest in showing current project information).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>4. Cookiefree Web Analytics (Matomo)</h2>
      <p>
        This website uses the <strong>cookiefree</strong> web analytics software <strong>Matomo</strong> (endpoint: matomo.silvio-und-maik.de) to produce aggregated, anonymous usage statistics. No cookies are set and no information is stored on or read from your device; because tracking is enforced cookieless and the IP address is truncated, § 25 TDDDG requires neither consent nor a cookie banner. Recorded are, among others, the IP address truncated to two bytes (the last two bytes are zeroed), browser and device type, screen resolution, language, region of origin, the referrer and the subpages visited, plus anonymous technical performance metrics (Core Web Vitals: loading time, layout stability, response time). There is <strong>no profiling</strong>, no cross-device tracking and no sharing with third parties; the data cannot be linked to individuals. The full IP address is used briefly on arrival of the request for location derivation (country and region), and only the truncated address (two bytes) is stored afterwards. Raw data is deleted after 180 days; aggregated reports without personal reference are kept. The app's usage statistics run on the same server (section&nbsp;15), in a separate measurement area. No external fonts or CDN services are loaded; all other resources are served locally from GitHub Pages. Legal basis: Art.&nbsp;6(1)(f) GDPR (legitimate interest in needs-driven improvement). You may object to this processing at any time (Art. 21 GDPR). Tick the opt-out option in the field below.
      </p>
      <p>
        This website sets no cookies. In browser storage (<code>localStorage</code>/<code>sessionStorage</code>) we keep only what a feature you use needs: your chosen language, whether the automatic language redirect already happened in this session, and whether you have already opened or closed the contact notice (so it does not appear again). This stays on your device and is never sent to any server; it is strictly necessary for the feature you asked for (Section&nbsp;25(2) no.&nbsp;2 TDDDG). You can delete it at any time in your browser settings.
      </p>
      <div id="matomo-opt-out-en"></div>
      <script is:inline src="https://matomo.silvio-und-maik.de/index.php?module=CoreAdminHome&action=optOutJS&divId=matomo-opt-out-en&language=en&showIntro=1"></script>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>5. External Links</h2>
      <p>
        This website contains links to external services:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>GitHub</strong> — Source code repository and releases</li>
        <li><strong>GitHub Sponsors / Ko-fi / Buy Me a Coffee</strong> — Voluntary support options</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When clicking these links, the respective privacy policies of the third-party providers apply. WhisPaste has no influence on data processing by these services.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>6. Core Principle</h2>
      <p>
        WhisPaste is designed with <strong>privacy first</strong>. Your <strong>audio and transcribed text never leave your device</strong> unless you explicitly choose a cloud service for transcription or for Smart Mode. By default (opt-out) the app sends two separately switchable data streams to guide development: <strong>usage statistics</strong> to a server we operate, and <strong>crash reports</strong> via Sentry (EU data region). Neither ever contains your audio, transcribed text, history or name. Crash reports are sent only for crashes, hangs of the app lasting 5&nbsp;seconds or more, and serious errors. Details in section&nbsp;15.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>7. Audio Recording &amp; Transcription</h2>
      <p>
        WhisPaste records audio from your microphone when you activate recording (via hotkey or button). The recorded audio is processed in one of two ways, depending on your settings:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Local / Offline transcription:</strong> Audio is processed entirely on your device using a local AI model: Whisper (via whisper.cpp) or Parakeet (via sherpa-onnx or whisper.cpp backends), depending on your settings. No audio data leaves your computer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloud transcription (OpenAI or Deepgram):</strong> Audio is sent to the servers of whichever provider you choose. This requires you to provide your own API key for that provider. The audio is transmitted directly from your device to the provider; WhisPaste does not route, store, or process it on any intermediary server of ours.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Smart Mode (optional text cleanup):</strong> Off by default. When it runs locally, the transcribed text is processed on your device with a downloaded language model. If you choose OpenAI as the Smart Mode service, the transcribed text and the selected preset's instructions are sent directly to OpenAI using your own OpenAI API key.
      </p>
      <p>
        When using a cloud provider, that provider's data processing terms apply: <a href="https://openai.com/policies/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAI Privacy Policy</a> or <a href="https://deepgram.com/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Deepgram Privacy Policy</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>8. Transcription History</h2>
      <p>
        Transcribed text is stored locally on your device in an SQLite database (<code>%APPDATA%\WhisPaste\</code> on Windows, <code>~/Library/Application Support/WhisPaste</code> on macOS, <code>~/.config/whispaste</code> on Linux). This data never leaves your device. You can view, search, export, and delete your transcription history at any time through the application.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Notes and note images:</strong> WhisPaste also stores your notes locally on your device, together with their version history (up to 50 versions and 4&nbsp;MiB in total per note; older versions are then removed automatically). Images you add to a note (via the file picker, by pasting from the clipboard, or by drag and drop) are stored by WhisPaste in the application folder; each note may hold up to 20 images and 32&nbsp;MiB, with 512&nbsp;MiB in total. Before saving, WhisPaste removes extra information from images, including GPS location, EXIF, XMP, IPTC and comment data; the image orientation is kept. Notes and images never leave your device.
      </p>
      <p>
        The PIN for your history also protects your notes: while the app is locked, notes and search show no content; you can still capture quick notes, but existing content is not shown. If you forget the PIN and reset it, all recordings, all notes with their version history and all note images are deleted immediately and permanently. Settings, snippets, and replacements are kept.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>9. API Key Storage</h2>
      <p>
        API keys for cloud providers (OpenAI, Deepgram) are <strong>not</strong> stored in plain text in a configuration file. They are kept in your operating system's secure credential store: Windows Credential Manager, the macOS Keychain, or libsecret on Linux. A key is transmitted <strong>only</strong> directly to that provider's API endpoint when you perform a transcription or Smart Mode request. WhisPaste does not send your API key to any other service or server.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>10. Configuration &amp; Local Storage</h2>
      <p>
        All other application settings (language, hotkey, theme, model preferences, etc.) are stored locally in the same application folder as your transcription history (see section&nbsp;8). Settings themselves are never transmitted. The one exception: usage statistics report the name of a changed setting, never its value (see section&nbsp;15).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Clipboard:</strong> The app reads the clipboard only on your device, to paste a dictation and then restore what was there before, and for the clipboard history, which is kept in memory only. Clipboard content is never written to disk and never transmitted. WhisPaste does not write dictation or clipboard content to the log. Where an error message could contain such content, only the error type is logged; every log line is also stripped of paths and secrets.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>11. AI Model Downloads</h2>
      <p>
        For local/offline transcription, WhisPaste downloads AI models from <strong>Hugging Face</strong>: the Whisper and Parakeet models as well as the optional Smart Mode language model come from separate repositories there. A corrupted Whisper model is re-downloaded automatically. During a model download, a standard HTTPS request is made to Hugging Face's servers. No personal data or identifiers are sent; only the model file URL is requested. Downloaded models are stored locally on your device.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>12. Auto-Update</h2>
      <p>
        The update mechanism depends on how you installed WhisPaste:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Installed via the <strong>Microsoft Store</strong>, the <strong>Mac App Store</strong>, <strong>Homebrew</strong>, or <strong>Scoop</strong>: the respective store or package manager handles update checks; the app itself performs no separate check. (Snap and Flatpak are planned but not yet available.)</li>
        <li>Installed via the <strong>Windows installer</strong> (incl. portable build) or the <strong>macOS DMG</strong> downloaded directly from GitHub (outside the Mac App Store): the app checks for a new version at startup and every 24&nbsp;hours afterwards through signed appcast feeds fetched from GitHub.</li>
        <li>On <strong>Linux</strong> (AppImage/.deb): the check queries the GitHub Releases API directly.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These requests contain no personal data; they simply fetch the latest release version information. No user identifiers, device information, or usage data are transmitted. Automatic checks can be switched off under <strong>Settings → Updates</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>13. Third-Party Data Sharing</h2>
      <p>
        WhisPaste does <strong>not share your content with third parties</strong>. Audio or text is sent to a cloud provider only when you explicitly choose one (OpenAI or Deepgram for transcription, OpenAI for Smart Mode), directly from your device under that provider's terms. Anonymous usage statistics go to a Matomo server operated by us (not a third party). Crash reports are processed by Sentry (EU data region) as a data processor under a data processing agreement (see section&nbsp;15). Both can be switched off (see section&nbsp;15).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>14. Network Connections</h2>
      <p>
        WhisPaste only makes network connections for the following purposes:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>OpenAI / Deepgram API — when cloud transcription is enabled (user-initiated)</li>
        <li>OpenAI API — only if you choose OpenAI as the Smart Mode service (user-initiated)</li>
        <li>Your own OpenAI-compatible server (e.g. LM Studio, Ollama, or a provider you choose) — only if you enter it for speech recognition or Smart Mode (user-initiated). Addresses on your device or in your local network stay there; any other address receives the data</li>
        <li>Hugging Face — when downloading offline AI models (user-initiated)</li>
        <li>GitHub — for automatic update checks (Releases API or appcast; on by default, can be switched off)</li>
        <li>Matomo — usage statistics (on by default, can be switched off, see section 15)</li>
        <li>Sentry — crash reports (on by default, can be switched off, see section 15)</li>
        <li>Supabase — only when you submit the optional feedback form (see section 16)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Connections to services on the internet use <strong>HTTPS</strong> encryption. A server of your own on your device or in your local network may use unencrypted HTTP; the app then shows a warning. Aside from the update check and the usage statistics and crash reports described in section 15, no background network activity occurs. A complete technical list of every connection is in <a href="https://github.com/whispaste/whispaste/blob/main/NETWORK.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NETWORK.md</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>15. Pseudonymous Usage Statistics &amp; Crash Reports</h2>
      <p>
        To guide development, WhisPaste collects two separate data streams. Both are on by default (opt-out) — the Privacy step of the onboarding says so as well. Both work without cookies, never contain audio, text, history or clipboard content, and can each be switched off under <strong>Settings → Privacy</strong>. The switches there are called <strong>"Help make WhisPaste better"</strong> (usage statistics) and <strong>"Send crash reports"</strong> (crash reports).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Usage statistics (Matomo).</strong> The app uses the same self-hosted Matomo server in the EU as the website (section&nbsp;4), in a separate measurement area. Only aggregated event counters and fixed categories are sent: app version, operating system, selected speech-recognition provider, install channel, interface language, update channel, the result of the update check, onboarding steps without content, and the names of changed settings (never their values). When statistics are on, these are added: the chosen speech model as a fixed name (small, medium, large-v3-turbo, Parakeet or cloud), coarse hardware classes (memory tier, GPU vendor, Mac chip family, compute backend), coarse speed classes, success or failure of model downloads with an error class, whether you kept the recommended model, and the time since onboarding as a coarse bucket. Raw data with a truncated IP address is deleted after 180 days; aggregated reports without personal reference are kept.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Onboarding answers.</strong> During onboarding you can also optionally say what you dictate for (fixed categories such as writing, development or education) and how you found WhisPaste. Only the fixed ID of your selection is sent. Whether you changed your first name is reported as a state (kept, edited, cleared), never the name itself. There is no separate switch for this: the answers run under the usage-statistics switch and are only sent while it is on.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Legal basis</strong> for both streams is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR. Our legitimate interest is the stability and development of the app. We have weighed this: only aggregated counters, fixed categories and error data without content are collected; the retention periods are short; and each stream can be switched off at any time. In our assessment, your interests therefore do not override this interest.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Crash reports (Sentry, EU data region).</strong> Only crashes, hangs of the app lasting 5&nbsp;seconds or more, and serious errors are sent. There is no performance measurement and no session tracking. Content: error message and stack trace, app version, operating system and CPU architecture, install channel, the most recent app log lines (WhisPaste does not write dictation or clipboard content to the log, see section&nbsp;10), and a pseudonymous, random per-install identifier of 12 characters. It is created on the first launch, is not derived from the computer, and is reset by deleting its file. Sentry does not store IP addresses, and the app sends no additional personal data by default. Before sending, the app replaces the user name in local file paths with a placeholder. Events containing secrets such as API keys are dropped; screenshots and on-screen content are never sent. A single error is reported at most three times, and one session sends at most 30 reports in total. Error data is kept for 30 days. Sentry processes it as a processor under a data processing agreement (DPA). To safeguard the transfer to the USA, where the parent company is based, the standard contractual clauses (SCC) under Art. 46(2)(c) GDPR or the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework apply, as for GitHub in section&nbsp;3. Switching crash reports off stops Sentry immediately, including crash and hang reports, and later launches do not start it at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pseudonymous identifiers on your device.</strong> The installation identifier for crash reports and the salt for the weekly value are stored locally. This is technically necessary for the feature that is switched on (Art.&nbsp;25(2) no.&nbsp;2 TDDDG), so no separate consent is required for it. For week-level analysis a locally generated marker is sent: a hash of a random device value that is never transmitted and of the calendar week. It changes every week and is not a persistent identifier; when usage statistics are off, it is neither generated nor sent. Each app start also gets a random visitor ID that is never stored and only links events from the same run.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Never</strong> transmitted, in either stream: your audio, transcribed text, clipboard content, history, snippets, tags, notes, API keys, or your name. Usage statistics also never include concrete hotkeys, file paths, cursor position or the target application.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Objection.</strong> You can object to either stream at any time (Art. 21 GDPR) by switching it off under <strong>Settings → Privacy</strong>. When usage statistics are off, the optional onboarding answers are not sent either.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>16. Feedback Form</h2>
      <p>
        WhisPaste includes an optional, voluntary feedback form in the app. Submitting it sends your rating, feedback text, chosen category, app version, a hashed, non-identifying device identifier, and the app's UI language to a Supabase database that we operate. Only if you choose to fill them in, a contact email address and a preferred contact language are also sent, so we can reply to your feedback. Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR (consent, given by actively submitting the form). None of this data is transmitted unless you submit the form.
      </p>
      <p>
        In addition, you can explicitly tick a box in the form to allow your feedback text to appear anonymously as a quote on this website. The box appears only when you choose the type Praise, because quotes are written like a rating: what you like and where there is still room to improve. Bug reports and feature wishes are never published as quotes. The box is unticked by default. Only your text and rating are then shown, never your name or email address, and only after our editorial review. Legal basis: Art.&nbsp;6(1)(a) GDPR. As evidence of your consent we store the time it was given (set by our server) and the version of the consent text you saw.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Withdrawing in the app:</strong> When you consent, the app creates a random withdrawal code that stays on your device, in the operating system's credential store (Keychain, Windows Credential Manager or the Secret Service on Linux; if none is available, in the app's settings). We only receive a hash of this code (SHA-256), from which the code cannot be recovered; the hash alone cannot withdraw the consent either. We store it with your feedback entry, at most until your withdrawal reaches us. Without a withdrawal we delete it with the entry: after 90&nbsp;days or, if we approved your feedback as a quote, when we delete the entry. "Withdraw consent" in the feedback section withdraws every consent you gave from this device with one click. On the device the withdrawal takes effect at once. It is sent to us once WhisPaste runs with an internet connection: right when you click, at the next app start, when you open the feedback section and, while the app is running, at growing intervals of at most one hour, for up to 30&nbsp;days in total. Until our server confirms receipt, the app shows the withdrawal as "being sent", then as "Withdrawal sent". If it cannot be delivered, because our server rejects it as invalid or after 30&nbsp;days without success, the app says so and points you to withdrawing by email. Once our server receives the withdrawal, it revokes your consent and the approval of the quote, deletes the hash and records the time of the withdrawal. The times of consent and withdrawal serve as evidence until deletion. Our daily clean-up deletes the feedback entry at the latest 90&nbsp;days after your feedback arrived, and in any case at the latest 90&nbsp;days after the withdrawal arrived. If your feedback is already older than 90&nbsp;days when the withdrawal arrives, the next daily clean-up deletes it, possibly well before 90&nbsp;days after the withdrawal. For these server deadlines, our server's time at each arrival counts. As a guideline, the quote disappears from the website within about 24&nbsp;hours, at the next daily update of the website; if an update fails, it can take longer.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Withdrawing by email:</strong> If you gave your consent on another device, reinstalled the app, no withdrawal code is stored on this device for another reason, or the app could not deliver the withdrawal, withdraw by email to <a href="mailto:kontakt@whispaste.de">kontakt@whispaste.de</a>. The app offers a prefilled email for this. If the app knows the date of your last feedback, it adds it automatically; for every undelivered withdrawal it also adds the hash described above as a withdrawal reference, never the withdrawal code itself. This lets us find the entry and revoke the consent by hand; the same deletion deadlines as for an in-app withdrawal then apply. We keep your withdrawal email as evidence that we carried out the withdrawal and delete it from our mailbox at the latest 90&nbsp;days after it arrived, the same period as for the feedback entry (storage limitation, Art.&nbsp;5(1)(e) GDPR). You send the email through your own email program and email provider to the operator of WhisPaste. Its transport is not end-to-end encrypted; we cannot influence its route through your provider. The withdrawal reference is a hash from which the withdrawal code cannot be recovered. Apart from this withdrawal reference, the email contains only the date of your last feedback, if the app knows it. If the app finds no email program, it shows the address and offers to copy the address and the message. The app deletes an undelivered withdrawal and its code from your device once you confirm that you sent the email, and at the latest 90&nbsp;days after your withdrawal. This deadline on the device counts from the moment you clicked "Withdraw consent" on your device, by your device's clock, not from its arrival with us. So that the app can offer you the withdrawal reference even while the credential store cannot be read, it keeps the hashes of your consents and the status of their withdrawals in its settings, never the withdrawal code. This information stays on your device only and is not part of any settings export. For a consent it is kept for as long as the consent stands. After a withdrawal the app deletes it once our server confirms the withdrawal or you confirm that you sent the email, and at the latest 90&nbsp;days after your withdrawal, even if it could not be delivered. Withdrawal does not affect the lawfulness of publication before it (Art.&nbsp;7(3) GDPR).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>17. Website Contact Form</h2>
      <p>
        Using the contact button in the bottom right corner (“Write to us”) you can voluntarily send us a message. We then process the type of request you chose, your message, your email address for the reply, and the language and path of the page you write from. No name or account is needed. The <strong>sole purpose</strong> is to answer your request. The legal basis is Art.&nbsp;6(1)(b) GDPR where your request concerns your use of WhisPaste, otherwise Art.&nbsp;6(1)(f) GDPR (our legitimate interest in answering requests).
      </p>
      <p>
        The data is sent TLS-encrypted to our interface <code>api.whispaste.de</code>. It runs on servers of <strong>netcup GmbH</strong> (Daimlerstraße 25, 76185 Karlsruhe, Germany), which acts as our processor under Art.&nbsp;28 GDPR; processing takes place in Germany. The interface forwards your message as an email to our mailbox and does not store its content itself. We delete the email once your request is resolved, at the latest after 12&nbsp;months, unless statutory retention obligations apply.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Abuse protection:</strong> To keep the form from being used for spam, we limit the number of messages per sender. For this we never store your IP address or email address in plain text, only as a hash keyed with a secret, and only until the respective time window has passed (at most 24&nbsp;hours, after which the entry is deleted automatically). A hidden check field and the time taken to fill in the form help detect automated submissions; no further data is collected for this. The interface's technical log contains no IP address, email address or message text, only the type and outcome of the request, and is deleted after 14&nbsp;days. The legal basis is Art.&nbsp;6(1)(f) GDPR (protection against abuse).
      </p>
      <p>
        No form data is transmitted unless you submit it. You can object to this processing at any time (Art.&nbsp;21 GDPR) and ask us to delete your message; write to the address given in the <a href="/en/impressum/">imprint</a>. The “Open the idea board” button leads to our idea board on <strong>VotePit</strong> (app.votepit.com), a separately operated service with its own privacy policy; no data flows there unless you open the link.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>18. Children's Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        WhisPaste does not knowingly collect data from children. Since the application does not collect any personal data or require account creation, there are no age-related data concerns.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>19. Changes to This Policy</h2>
      <p>
        This privacy policy may be updated to reflect changes in the application. Significant changes will be noted in the application's release notes on GitHub.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>20. Contact</h2>
      <p>
        For privacy-related questions or concerns, please refer to the <a href="/en/impressum/">Legal Notice (Impressum)</a> page for contact information.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>21. Your Rights</h2>
      <p>
        You have the right at any time to:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Access your stored data (Art. 15 GDPR)</li>
        <li>Rectification of inaccurate data (Art. 16 GDPR)</li>
        <li>Erasure of your data (Art. 17 GDPR)</li>
        <li>Restriction of processing (Art. 18 GDPR)</li>
        <li>Data portability (Art. 20 GDPR)</li>
        <li>Object to processing (Art. 21 GDPR)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Which data we hold depends on what you use: usage statistics (sections 4 and 15) are stored only pseudonymously and without names, so we usually cannot link them to you (Art. 11 GDPR). Crash reports (section 15) are deleted after 30 days. If you submitted a form (sections 16 and 17), write to us and we will delete your details. For requests regarding GitHub server logs, please contact <a href="https://support.github.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Support</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        You also have the right to lodge a complaint with a data protection supervisory authority (Art. 77 GDPR).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>22. SSL/TLS Encryption</h2>
      <p>
        This website uses SSL/TLS encryption (HTTPS) for security reasons. An encrypted connection is indicated by the address bar changing from "http://" to "https://".
      </p>
    </section>
</LegalPage>
